{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it","id":1161217,"title":"The Digital Web - How Non-Virtual Adepts Interact With It","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-21T11:11:20-08:00","id":1161217,"title":"The Digital Web - How Non-Virtual Adepts Interact With It","content":"It's easy to assume that only the Virtual Adepts and Technocrat Conventions like the New World Order and Iteration X interact meaningfully with the Digital Web. This, however, seems like a mistaken notion to me, though not an un-intuitive one. An entire section of Reality, and yet only these few groups explore it? Unlikely. If nothing else, even most predominantly mystical groups have their technomantic adherents.<br /> <br /> So I want to know: how would all the various other groups deal with the Digital Web, and how would their different Paradigms, Practices, and Instruments change that?<br /> <br /> How does an Etherite supercomputer differ from a Virtual Adept one? How do the Knights Templar use holy hacking to defend Christendom on the internet? Did the magi of House Thig try to realize the city of Pymander in virtual space (and if so, what remains of their efforts following the House's dissolution and consolidation in House Verditus)? Would a Progenitor have interest in wholly digital lifeforms? Are the gleaming towers of the modern Taftani defended from cyber attack by digital djinn?<br /> <br /> And how do non-mages deal with the Digital Web? Remember, anyone with sufficient knowledge and equipment can interact with it on a basic level, regardless of Awakening. How, then, do Glass Walker werewolves utilize the space? What is the presence of Shreknet within the Digital Web, and how do Nosferatu adapt to the plane and its added dimension of complexity? If a person dies and makes a Wraith, can they potentially leave a literal ghost in the machine?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T14:36:18-08:00","id":1161283,"title":"","content":"I've no idea what M20 has to say about it, but in 2nd and Rev editions, all the Traditions had groups looking at the Digital Web to one degree or another. Obviously the Virtual Adepts were kings of the mountain, so to speak, with the Etherites a respectable second, but the others all had technomancer individuals or factions who would pop up there from time to time. Unfortunately, not a lot of them got any exploration or detail, beyond general descriptions in the Tradition books. To that end, over the years, I've invented (or re-appropriated) some of my own. <br /> <br /> <i>The Army of the Electric Monkey Prince</i> (Akashic Brotherhood, part of the Li Hai), founded in the 1970s by the self-proclaimed Electric Monkey Prince, an anarchist gang of computer hackers, modern artists and tech otaku trying to diffuse Akashic philosophy through the internet, underground music scenes and guerrilla performance art, as well as any other out of the box medium they can come up with. This includes exploring, formatting and exploiting the Digital Web in search of enlightenment and universal benevolence. <br /> <br /> <i>Dues Ex Machina</i> (Celestial Chorus, part of the Alexandrian Society), ultramodernist techno-gnostics, a collective of coders, mathematicians, engineers and physicists seeking the voice of The One within the Digital Web, while also working to use the Internet and other modern communication and media systems to advance the interests of the Celestial Chorus and spread it's message of universal faith, harmony and light. <br /> <br /> <i>Bongo's Rangers</i> (Cult of Ecstasy, part of the Sowers of Discord), disciples of the 1970s Ecstatic punk rock god Deepok Bongo, pursuing high-tech anarchism via wild outlaw raves, bleeding edge computer terrorism and chemically engineered designer drugs that will tie your brain in knots while showing you the face of God and the true secrets of Games of Divinity. To them, the Digital Web is simultaneously beautiful raw chaos of unlimited potential and a brutal no holds barred war zone against those who would attempt to corrupt or chain that potential. <br /> <br /> <i>The Dissonance Society</i> (Cult of Ecstasy, part of the Sowers of Discord), a collective of radical libertarian and anarchist philosophers originating in the the Salons of the Enlightenment, coming to their current fruition through Bohemian and Beatnik culture, East Coast Intellectualism and Leary's psychedelic experiments. They fight the Ascension War in the realm of ideas, writing articles on various socio-political topics, relentlessly challenging the status quo, group think and statist regulation of the individual. The Internet is seen as a vital frontier and battle ground for freedom of the mind, and they typically will enter the Digital Web as part of LSD psychonaut experiments. <br /> <br /> <i>Gutenberg's Children</i> (Dreamspeakers, part of the Baruti), artists, storytellers and collectors who publish their works through modern mass media, especially counterculture publishing ventures that allow them to bypass the gatekeepers of public discourse. Members create their own novels, albums, manga, films and other mediums, imbuing them with powerful mental and spiritual effects. The Internet is just the latest way to self-publish, and many see the Digital Web as having the potential to house the greatest interactive collection of stories in human history. <br /> <br /> <i>The Men of Letters</i> (Dreamspeakers, part of the Ghost Wheel Society), long buried in the worlds of academia, business and government, these shamans of bureaucracy pass themselves off as mundane intellectuals while investigating the spiritual power of words, written language and concepts, turning the spirits of language and communication against the Technocracy. A growing number have found great joy in exploring the potential of computer languages and the Internet, often undertaking vision quests into the Digital Web to search for powerful computer spirits. <br /> <br /> <i>The Digital Dream</i> (Dreamspeakers, part of the Spirit Smiths), computer nerds and IT specialists who build their own fetish computers and write mystic code, communicating with modern spirits of technology, communication and artificial materials. They view the Internet and the Digital Web as new &quot;tribes&quot; and &quot;nations&quot; that are desperately in need of their own shaman to provide guidance, healing and protection from evil. <br /> <br /> <i>House Thig</i> (Order of Hermes), using modern tools and symbols to work ancient ritual magic, and seen as bordering on blasphemy by arch-traditionalists for doing so, members substitute lawyers, guns and money for hierophants, swords and gold, and prefer computers to written grimoires and coding to Latin. The House is built on a triangle of strict personal discipline, obsessive collection of new information, and the ruthless preservation of personal face/reputation. Masters of modern elementalism and Ars Cupidae, they believe they bring a &quot;punk rock&quot; sensibility to the Order. To them, the Digital Web is a place of great potential, one that they feel they alone among the Order are capable of understanding and making use of. It is a raw reality waiting for one with the will to shape it, and can potentially be the place Pymander can be realized. <br /> <br /> <i>Sisterhood of the Moon</i> (Verbena, part of the Moonseekers), modern neo-pagans, New Agers and Wiccans, they take a strong interest in community issues, politics and social causes. Famed for their savvy with computers, alternative media and counterculture, as well as their openness to technomancy, the Sisterhood believes in nurturing the Tree of Life by bringing people together, especially those who might otherwise feel excluded by mainstream society. The Internet has been a great boon to their work, and it is only natural that many enjoy exploring the untapped possibilities of the Digital Web, and seeing how the Tree of Life manifests within it. <br /> <br /> <i>The Spider's Web</i> (Hollow Ones, part of the Unseen Society), gothic technomancers blending strange and esoteric arts with computers and other high tech devices, delving into the darker corners of the Internet and modern cyberculture, looking to unlock hidden doors and unearth buried secrets (many of which might prefer to remain locked and buried) using self-scripted programs, homemade gadgets, invented spells and unusual spirit aides. Those that find their way onto the Digital Web take to it like a fish to water, eager to explore, poke and prod to their dark heart's content. <br /> <br /> As for other supernaturals, Glasswalkers (and other Garou or Fera) who find the Digital Web tend to do so via the Umbral Realm called the Cyber Realm. I think, but can't be certain, that guidelines for that were in either Digital Web 2.0 or Umbra Revised. Vampires likely need to use Auspex to actually project into the Web. Again, I think Digital Web 2.0 may have covered this. (Maybe?). Wraith's can enter computer networks using the Inhabit Arcanos. The Guild specializing in said power, the Artificers, have an entire faction devoted to mucking around with computers and their Guildbook includes alternate powers for Inhabit that make this even easier. I don't think Changeling ever really got around to discussing computers that much.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T17:27:53-08:00","id":1161347,"title":"","content":"While I suppose Auspex could allow a vampire a more immersive dive into the Digital Web (and the Hacktivist Thaumaturges probably have some kind of Combination Discipline between Auspex and the Path of Technomancy that does similar), neither is strictly necessary to interact with the Digital Web. As I said before, anyone can access the place (in a sensory manner, anyway) if they have the right knowledge and technology. As of M20, that technology has become all the more common, what with the resurgence of VR. So it's entirely possible that forward-thinking, tech-savvy kindred are already taking their first steps into the Digital Web.<br /> <br /> They just don't have as an immersive experience as Mages do, what with the detached nature of purely mundane VR exploration. And those Kindred are way behind Mage groups in their understanding of the realm.<br /> <br /> <br /> On another note, I'm honestly wondering what, if anything, differentiates the Cyber Realm werewolves visit and the Digital Web. Personally, I don't think there is a difference; rather, they are the same place, just accessed through different means.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T19:05:59-08:00","id":1161382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1161283#post1161283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>Dues Ex Machina</i> (Celestial Chorus, part of the Alexandrian Society), ultramodernist techno-gnostics, a collective of coders, mathematicians, engineers and physicists seeking the voice of The One within the Digital Web, while also working to use the Internet and other modern communication and media systems to advance the interests of the Celestial Chorus and spread it's message of universal faith, harmony and light....<br /> <br /> As for other supernaturals, Glasswalkers (and other Garou or Fera) who find the Digital Web tend to do so via the Umbral Realm called the Cyber Realm. I think, but can't be certain, that guidelines for that were in either Digital Web 2.0 or Umbra Revised. Vampires likely need to use Auspex to actually project into the Web. Again, I think Digital Web 2.0 may have covered this. (Maybe?). Wraith's can enter computer networks using the Inhabit Arcanos. The Guild specializing in said power, the Artificers, have an entire faction devoted to mucking around with computers and their Guildbook includes alternate powers for Inhabit that make this even easier. I don't think Changeling ever really got around to discussing computers that much.</div> </div> </div> </div>If we're accepting WTA cosmology, Then the Chorusers are practically Begging to get themselves One Songed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T06:02:36-08:00","id":1161487,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1161347#post1161347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On another note, I'm honestly wondering what, if anything, differentiates the Cyber Realm werewolves visit and the Digital Web. Personally, I don't think there is a difference; rather, they are the same place, just accessed through different means.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're connected but they're very different.<br /> <br /> The Cyberrealm it's what Traditionalist mages call Dystopia (book of worlds), it's a middle umbra reflection of the technology and development of humanity. All of it, not just internet, travel or &quot;information&quot;.<br /> Old fashined Traditionalists have a rather gloomy perception of it (that supersedes Cyberrealm and Scar), hence the name, but it's mostly the same place. It has the slums where discarded tech and &quot;poor&quot; spirits thrive, while Entropy gnaws at them (there's little digital stuff there), and the tall buildings where &quot;rich&quot; spirits live and everything it's bleeding edge new: you can enter Werewolf version of the Digital Web from there.<br /> <br /> This version of the Web it's &quot;the sprit of the internet&quot;, and it's deeply connected with Mage's Digital Web, but it's residents are Ephemera, while VA web residents are Forces. Also Garou need to enter specific portals that exist within the Realm to get to the Web, and viceversa. It's not just &quot;same place different perspective&quot; like when two mages go to the Umbra, but I agree that &quot;<i>this</i>&quot; place of the Cyberrealm has overlaping roles with the DigiWeb and could be merged.<br /> I think it could be said that this part of the realm it's akin to a middle umbral &quot;layer&quot; of the DigiWeb.<br /> <br /> Also, rumors say that the DigiWeb precedes the technology of the Interntet, being related to Mount Qaf and thus Correspondence. Werewolf understanding doesn't adress that connection. It could be that they just haven't discovered that the methaphysical importance of the Cyberrealm transcends humanity's tech-work, but I don't think so (Glasswalkers traveled to this realm before the web was invented, when it had other names).<br /> <br /> The first book about the Digital Web it's very informative about the differences betwen these Webs and what Realms are connected to VA DigiWeb (revised it's much more conservative about that). Supposedly Malkavian madness network it's also connected (to some extent), supporting the idea that the DigiWeb <i>it's realy about ALL connections</i>, unlike the Cyber-realm which centers itself around technology and human development during history.<br /> While human development includes things like comunication and roads (be they digital or physical), and thus it's likely that the Ciberrealm was always connected with the DigiWeb/Qaf, I think that the realms have different &quot;meanings&quot; and thus it's acceptable for them to be different places in the Umbra (albeit the &quot;internet&quot; aspect of both could be merged easily).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 11-22-2017, 09:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T13:33:29-08:00","id":1161598,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1161382#post1161382\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If we're accepting WTA cosmology, Then the Chorusers are practically Begging to get themselves One Songed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, from a Werewolf perspective, the majority of human religions are the work of the Patriarch, and the Celestial Chorus would certainly come across as Weaver-orriented (not as badly as the Cabal of Pure Thought, but still). Though, some - Glasswalkers or Children of Gaia, for example, or Black Furies in the Order of Our Merciful Mother - may see them as closer to what the Weaver/Patriarch is <i>supposed to be</i> rather than what it currently is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T13:45:48-08:00","id":1161603,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1161347#post1161347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I suppose Auspex could allow a vampire a more immersive dive into the Digital Web (and the Hacktivist Thaumaturges probably have some kind of Combination Discipline between Auspex and the Path of Technomancy that does similar), neither is strictly necessary to interact with the Digital Web. As I said before, anyone can access the place (in a sensory manner, anyway) if they have the right knowledge and technology. As of M20, that technology has become all the more common, what with the resurgence of VR. So it's entirely possible that forward-thinking, tech-savvy kindred are already taking their first steps into the Digital Web.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a really interesting issue of HELLBLAZER from back during the original Jamie Delano run in which a character is able to project his mind into computer networks, and it was partially adapted into one episode of the short lived Constantine TV series. I can't recall the exact specifics, but I think it involved a mix of self-hypnosis, alpha-wave synchronization and possibly psychoactive substances. It occurs to me that such a thing would make an interesting method for Toreador to enter the Digital Web (hypnotic patters of light on the screen that are designed to trip Auspex effects and the clan weakness. especially after drinking blood spiked with something to amp one's sensations even further).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T18:50:28-08:00","id":1161690,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1161603#post1161603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There's a really interesting issue of HELLBLAZER from back during the original Jamie Delano run in which a character is able to project his mind into computer networks, and it was partially adapted into one episode of the short lived Constantine TV series. I can't recall the exact specifics, but I think it involved a mix of self-hypnosis, alpha-wave synchronization and possibly psychoactive substances. It occurs to me that such a thing would make an interesting method for Toreador to enter the Digital Web (hypnotic patters of light on the screen that are designed to trip Auspex effects and the clan weakness. especially after drinking blood spiked with something to amp one's sensations even further).</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. Interesting. Kind of reminds me of the webcomic <a href=\"http://www.drugsandwires.fail/\" target=\"_blank\">Drugs &amp; Wires</a>, set in an alt-history version of the 90s, where cybernetics are more common. A subculture developed in the setting around using brain implants (repurposed from medical implants) to immerse themselves in simulations. Because of the limitations of the tech at the time, most users of such things boost the vividness of their &quot;trips&quot; using psychoactive drugs (hence the title).<br /> <br /> I can imagine a faction of Virtual Adepts, Sahajiya, or some combination of the two utilizing this sort of method to interact with the Digital Web.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T18:52:35-08:00","id":1161694,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1161487#post1161487\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They're connected but they're very different.<br /> <br /> The Cyberrealm it's what Traditionalist mages call Dystopia (book of worlds), it's a middle umbra reflection of the technology and development of humanity. All of it, not just internet, travel or &quot;information&quot;.<br /> Old fashined Traditionalists have a rather gloomy perception of it (that supersedes Cyberrealm and Scar), hence the name, but it's mostly the same place. It has the slums where discarded tech and &quot;poor&quot; spirits thrive, while Entropy gnaws at them (there's little digital stuff there), and the tall buildings where &quot;rich&quot; spirits live and everything it's bleeding edge new: you can enter Werewolf version of the Digital Web from there.<br /> <br /> This version of the Web it's &quot;the sprit of the internet&quot;, and it's deeply connected with Mage's Digital Web, but it's residents are Ephemera, while VA web residents are Forces. Also Garou need to enter specific portals that exist within the Realm to get to the Web, and viceversa. It's not just &quot;same place different perspective&quot; like when two mages go to the Umbra, but I agree that &quot;<i>this</i>&quot; place of the Cyberrealm has overlaping roles with the DigiWeb and could be merged.<br /> I think it could be said that this part of the realm it's akin to a middle umbral &quot;layer&quot; of the DigiWeb.<br /> <br /> Also, rumors say that the DigiWeb precedes the technology of the Interntet, being related to Mount Qaf and thus Correspondence. Werewolf understanding doesn't adress that connection. It could be that they just haven't discovered that the methaphysical importance of the Cyberrealm transcends humanity's tech-work, but I don't think so (Glasswalkers traveled to this realm before the web was invented, when it had other names).<br /> <br /> The first book about the Digital Web it's very informative about the differences betwen these Webs and what Realms are connected to VA DigiWeb (revised it's much more conservative about that). Supposedly Malkavian madness network it's also connected (to some extent), supporting the idea that the DigiWeb <i>it's realy about ALL connections</i>, unlike the Cyber-realm which centers itself around technology and human development during history.<br /> While human development includes things like comunication and roads (be they digital or physical), and thus it's likely that the Ciberrealm was always connected with the DigiWeb/Qaf, I think that the realms have different &quot;meanings&quot; and thus it's acceptable for them to be different places in the Umbra (albeit the &quot;internet&quot; aspect of both could be merged easily).</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you. This post was very informative. Consider me schooled proper.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-23T21:38:55-08:00","id":1162091,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1161598#post1161598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, from a Werewolf perspective, the majority of human religions are the work of the Patriarch, and the Celestial Chorus would certainly come across as Weaver-orriented (not as badly as the Cabal of Pure Thought, but still). Though, some - Glasswalkers or Children of Gaia, for example, or Black Furies in the Order of Our Merciful Mother - may see them as closer to what the Weaver/Patriarch is <i>supposed to be</i> rather than what it currently is.</div> </div> </div> </div>I still imagine a choruser coming across knowledge of the Patriarch/One Song will put them in an awkward position alongside some Very uncomfortable questions. Especially with the coincidence of the phrases &quot;Voice of the One&quot; and &quot;One Song&quot;(and in a crossover I'd leave them as coincidences, but the character themselves won't know that)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-24T05:13:52-08:00","id":1162150,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1161217-the-digital-web-how-non-virtual-adepts-interact-with-it?p=1162091#post1162091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I still imagine a choruser coming across knowledge of the Patriarch/One Song will put them in an awkward position alongside some Very uncomfortable questions.<b> Especially with the coincidence of the phrases &quot;Voice of the One&quot; and &quot;One Song&quot;</b>(and in a crossover I'd leave them as coincidences, but the character themselves won't know that)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's very true. :P <br /> \u200b<br /> Although, in my opinion, Choristers seem more attuned with the High Umbra angelics and demonics, probably missing many Weaver-related phenomena. Not that they aren't related, but concentrating in different &quot;layers&quot; of Reality(tm) may be what prevents Chorus from being full of Drones. It may be an interesting issue for a Kinfolk Chorister that's deliberately trying to &quot;get&quot; the cosmology of their cousins to use it with her magick-discovering Weaver influence in religion would be almost natural if the Chorister focuses that way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}